The page belongs to Grothendieck's flag-based ('repères') formalism for combinatorial maps ('cartes'), which he uses to set up dessins d'enfants and the Teichmüller tower.

**The figure.** It shows a 2-cell complex K (thick lines) on a surface together with its barycentric subdivision. Each small triangle (a flag, 'repère') r has three vertices: S^0_r = s, a map vertex (type 0); S^1_r = ã, an edge midpoint (type 1); and S^∞_r = f̃, a face centre (type ∞, or 2).

**The reflections τ_i.** For i ∈ {0, 1, ∞}, τ_i r is the flag adjacent to r across the side opposite the type-i vertex. So τ_i changes the type-i vertex and fixes the other two; the margin note says this of σ_i. These generate the cartographic group C_2 = ⟨τ_0, τ_1, τ_∞ | τ_i² = 1, (τ_0 τ_∞)² = 1⟩, which acts on the flag set R(X).

**The rotations ρ_i.** Products of two reflections are rotations about the vertex of the remaining type:
- ρ_∞ = τ_1 τ_0 rotates about the face centre;
- ρ_0 = τ_∞ τ_1 rotates about the map vertex;
- ρ_1 = τ_0 τ_∞ rotates about the edge midpoint.

With these conventions ρ_∞ ρ_1 ρ_0 = 1. The rotations generate the orientation-preserving index-2 subgroup C_2^+ = ⟨ρ_0, ρ_1, ρ_∞ | ρ_1² = 1, ρ_∞ ρ_1 ρ_0 = 1⟩ ≅ Z * Z/2. By the previous page, finite C_2^+-sets are equivalent to oriented maps.

**Orientation conventions.** Two adjacent flags r and r' = τ_i r induce opposite local orientations of the surface. Grothendieck observes that nevertheless they induce the same orientation on their common side. These side orientations are always (0→1), (1→∞), (∞→0) on the sides 'of type ∞, 0, 1' respectively. By contrast, the senses of rotation they induce around their common vertices are opposite.

**The flag count.** The figure shows the flags reachable by words of length at most 2 in the τ_i:
- r;
- τ_0 r, τ_1 r, τ_∞ r;
- τ_1 τ_0 r = ρ_∞ r and τ_0 τ_1 r = ρ_∞^{-1} r;
- τ_0 τ_∞ r = τ_∞ τ_0 r = ρ_1 r = ρ_1^{-1} r;
- τ_∞ τ_1 r = ρ_0 r and τ_1 τ_∞ r = ρ_0^{-1} r.

That makes 1 + 3 + (2 + 1 + 2) = 9 flags. Without the relation τ_0 τ_∞ = τ_∞ τ_0, i.e. ρ_1² = 1 (an edge has exactly four flags), there would be 10.

**Purpose.** The page is an expository local picture fixing conventions (types, orientations, generators) for the equivalence 'oriented maps ≅ finite C_2^+-sets'. The next page uses these conventions for dual maps and for maps with folded edges ('arêtes pliées').
